        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: y = \\ln (2x+1).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "The chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like\n[MATH: y = \\ln (2x+1).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>First notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: y = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "First notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n[MATH: y = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:</p>",
            "readable_text": "We can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (dy)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = (d)/(du)(\\ln u) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(2x + 1); = (1)/(u) \u22c5 2; = (2)/(u)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (dy)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = (d)/(du)(\\ln u) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(2x + 1); = (1)/(u) \u22c5 2; = (2)/(u)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Substituting <span class=\"math\">[MATH: u=2x+1,]</span> we obtain the final result:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Substituting [MATH: u=2x+1,] we obtain the final result:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (2)/(2x + 1)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (2)/(2x + 1)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nThe chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like\n[MATH: y = \\ln (2x+1).]\n\nContent:\nFirst notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n[MATH: y = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1.]\n\nContent:\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (dy)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = (d)/(du)(\\ln u) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(2x + 1); = (1)/(u) \u22c5 2; = (2)/(u)]\n\nContent:\nSubstituting [MATH: u=2x+1,] we obtain the final result:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (2)/(2x + 1)]"
      },
