    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like\n[MATH: y = \\ln (2x+1).]\n\nContent:\nFirst notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n[MATH: y = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1.]\n\nContent:\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (dy)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = (d)/(du)(\\ln u) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(2x + 1); = (1)/(u) \u22c5 2; = (2)/(u)]\n\nContent:\nSubstituting [MATH: u=2x+1,] we obtain the final result:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (2)/(2x + 1)]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the derivative of [MATH: f(x) = \\ln(x^2-4).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we express [MATH: f(x)] as the composite function\n\n[MATH: f = \\ln u, \\qquad u = x^2-4.]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x.]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n[MATH: (df)/(dx)|= (df)/(du) \u22c5 (du)/(dx); = (1)/(u) \u22c5 2x; = (1)/(x^{2} \u2212 4) \u22c5 2x; = (2x)/(x^{2} \u2212 4).]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nFor [MATH: g(x) = \\ln (2x)], find [MATH: g^{\u2032}(x).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(x)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(2x)]\nc. [MATH: (2)/(x)]\nd. [MATH: (2)/(x) + 1]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(x) + 2]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: f(x) = \\ln (x^{3} \u2212 4)], find [MATH: (df)/(dx).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (x)/(x^{3} \u2212 4)]\nb. [MATH: (x^{2})/(x^{3} \u2212 4)]\nc. [MATH: (6)/(x^{3} \u2212 4)]\nd. [MATH: 2x\\ln (x^{3} \u2212 4)]\ne. [MATH: (3x^{2})/(x^{3} \u2212 4)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nIf [MATH: y = \\log_2(\\ln{x}),] what is [MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we express [MATH: y] as the composite function\n\n[MATH: y = \\log_2 u, \\qquad u = \\ln x.]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u\\ln 2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}.]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n[MATH: (dy)/(dx)|= (1)/(u\\ln 2) \u22c5 (1)/(x); = (1)/(xu\\ln 2); = (1)/(x\\ln x\\ln 2).]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: (dy)/(dx)] for [MATH: y = \\log_{3} (5x \u2212 2).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (5)/((5x \u2212 2)\\ln 5)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/((5x \u2212 2)\\ln 5)]\nc. [MATH: (5)/((2x \u2212 5)\\ln 3)]\nd. [MATH: (5)/((5x \u2212 2)\\ln 3)]\ne. [MATH: (5)/((5x \u2212 2))]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: (dy)/(dx)] for [MATH: y = \\log_{2} (sqrt(x)).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(2x\\ln 2)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(x\\ln 2)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(2sqrt(x)\\ln 2)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(2x)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(sqrt(x)\\ln 2)]"
